1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,390
Imagine someone alive
five thousand years ago.

2
00:00:04,144 --> 00:00:06,874
No electricity. No books. No schools.

3
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:10,850
By our standards, an
extraordinarily simple life.

4
00:00:11,653 --> 00:00:14,663
So you might expect an
extraordinarily simple language.

5
00:00:15,313 --> 00:00:18,283
Something like: I hunt.
You hunt. Tomorrow we hunt.

6
00:00:19,133 --> 00:00:20,843
That is not what we find.

7
00:00:21,767 --> 00:00:24,077
Latin, Old English, Old Norse.

8
00:00:24,947 --> 00:00:29,037
All of them had grammatical machinery
that modern English has mostly lost.

9
00:00:29,887 --> 00:00:33,157
And you don't need an ancient
language to see how odd this gets.

10
00:00:34,087 --> 00:00:35,537
Modern English will do.

11
00:00:36,299 --> 00:00:38,969
The verb is "go". The
past tense is "went".

12
00:00:39,851 --> 00:00:41,051
Why isn't it "goed"?

13
00:00:43,315 --> 00:00:45,525
Because "went" never belonged to "go".

14
00:00:46,375 --> 00:00:48,505
It was the past tense of a different verb.

15
00:00:49,561 --> 00:00:51,431
"Wend". To turn, to make your way.

16
00:00:51,941 --> 00:00:53,691
You can still wend your way home.

17
00:00:54,413 --> 00:00:56,103
Two verbs. Two meanings.

18
00:00:56,313 --> 00:00:59,073
And at some point English
quietly took the past

19
00:00:59,153 --> 00:01:01,603
tense of one of them and
handed it to the other.

20
00:01:02,297 --> 00:01:03,967
"Go" lost its own past.

21
00:01:04,717 --> 00:01:06,427
"Wend" grew a tidy new one: "wended".

22
00:01:07,835 --> 00:01:12,485
Today several hundred million people
simply learn it: go, went, gone.

23
00:01:12,975 --> 00:01:16,545
Nobody stops to think about the
history in the middle of a sentence.

24
00:01:17,427 --> 00:01:19,077
Nobody voted on that.

25
00:01:19,587 --> 00:01:21,437
Nobody wrote it down as a rule.

26
00:01:21,987 --> 00:01:23,597
It happened, and then it stayed.

27
00:01:24,439 --> 00:01:28,329
Which is this entire video,
hiding inside one irregular verb.

28
00:01:31,249 --> 00:01:35,059
Because the first mistake we make
is treating language as technology.

29
00:01:36,081 --> 00:01:40,271
When we meet a complicated machine,
we assume a simpler one came first.

30
00:01:41,126 --> 00:01:45,116
The earliest computers were
crude. Later ones were not.

31
00:01:45,706 --> 00:01:47,316
Every version adds capability.

32
00:01:48,394 --> 00:01:51,264
So it's tempting to tell
the same story about speech.

33
00:01:51,994 --> 00:01:53,524
Primitive sounds first.

34
00:01:54,234 --> 00:01:55,724
Grammar bolted on afterwards.

35
00:01:56,771 --> 00:01:58,601
But nobody designed English.

36
00:01:59,291 --> 00:02:00,801
Nobody designed Latin.

37
00:02:01,712 --> 00:02:03,972
There was no committee
sitting around three thousand

38
00:02:04,052 --> 00:02:07,582
years ago, agreeing that the
language needed more endings.

39
00:02:08,549 --> 00:02:11,819
Languages don't get
released. They get used.

40
00:02:12,658 --> 00:02:17,508
And every generation alters them slightly,
without meaning to. Sounds shift.

41
00:02:18,238 --> 00:02:20,548
Words drift. Endings wear down.

42
00:02:21,270 --> 00:02:23,480
Nobody is in charge of any of it.

43
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:28,570
Which is also how one
language turns into many.

44
00:02:29,353 --> 00:02:32,703
Take a population that all speaks
the same way, and split it.

45
00:02:33,493 --> 00:02:35,203
Half of them move somewhere else.

46
00:02:35,979 --> 00:02:37,609
At first, nothing happens.

47
00:02:38,279 --> 00:02:40,069
They still speak the same language.

48
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:42,750
Then the small changes begin.

49
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:46,930
A sound shifts on one side of
the mountain and not the other.

50
00:02:47,653 --> 00:02:51,283
A word picks up a new meaning
here and keeps the old one there.

51
00:02:52,153 --> 00:02:55,823
An ending survives in one group
and quietly dies in the second.

52
00:02:56,625 --> 00:03:00,795
And because the two groups are no longer
talking, none of it ever gets compared.

53
00:03:01,777 --> 00:03:04,567
After a few generations,
you notice an accent.

54
00:03:05,479 --> 00:03:08,249
After a few hundred years,
you notice the grammar.

55
00:03:09,137 --> 00:03:11,347
After a thousand, you
may need a translator.

56
00:03:12,668 --> 00:03:15,838
And at no point did anyone
decide to start a new language.

57
00:03:18,828 --> 00:03:21,548
So if languages are
always moving, why don't

58
00:03:21,628 --> 00:03:23,838
they simply wear down into something easy?

59
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:26,050
Sometimes they do.

60
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:28,470
English is the usual example.

61
00:03:29,369 --> 00:03:31,539
Old English had grammatical gender.

62
00:03:32,549 --> 00:03:34,569
Its nouns changed their endings depending

63
00:03:34,649 --> 00:03:36,899
on the job they were
doing in the sentence.

64
00:03:37,872 --> 00:03:39,582
Almost all of that is gone.

65
00:03:40,352 --> 00:03:43,022
A modern English noun
barely changes at all.

66
00:03:43,751 --> 00:03:45,251
Which really is simplification.

67
00:03:45,331 --> 00:03:47,421
In that corner of the grammar.

68
00:03:48,327 --> 00:03:51,777
But the information didn't
evaporate. It moved.

69
00:03:52,649 --> 00:03:53,959
"The dog bit the man."

70
00:03:54,849 --> 00:03:56,219
"The man bit the dog."

71
00:03:57,029 --> 00:03:58,819
Same words. Same endings.

72
00:03:59,369 --> 00:04:00,919
Entirely different afternoon.

73
00:04:02,527 --> 00:04:05,707
Old English had more freedom
in word order, because the

74
00:04:05,787 --> 00:04:08,837
endings carried information
about who did what to whom.

75
00:04:09,535 --> 00:04:11,605
Modern English has far less of it.

76
00:04:12,295 --> 00:04:15,985
Word order is holding that job
now, and it never gets a day off.

77
00:04:16,737 --> 00:04:18,897
Dropping one mechanism
and leaning harder on

78
00:04:18,977 --> 00:04:21,847
another is not the same
as getting simpler.

79
00:04:22,679 --> 00:04:24,949
And English didn't only lose things.

80
00:04:25,559 --> 00:04:27,699
While the endings were wearing
away, it was assembling

81
00:04:27,779 --> 00:04:31,089
something else: a small
army of helper verbs.

82
00:04:32,078 --> 00:04:36,008
"I go." "I am going." "I have
gone." "I had been going."

83
00:04:36,378 --> 00:04:37,948
"I am going to go."

84
00:04:38,429 --> 00:04:40,129
Modern English does
that work with auxiliary

85
00:04:40,209 --> 00:04:43,339
verbs, where Old English
leaned on endings.

86
00:04:44,209 --> 00:04:48,339
The endings left through one door, and
the auxiliaries walked in through another.

87
00:04:49,044 --> 00:04:52,494
Complexity rarely
disappears. It moves house.

88
00:04:55,054 --> 00:04:56,674
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

89
00:04:56,754 --> 00:04:59,604
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

90
00:05:00,494 --> 00:05:01,964
It really helps the channel.

91
00:05:03,347 --> 00:05:06,997
So how would you even decide that
one language is simpler than another?

92
00:05:07,688 --> 00:05:09,678
There is no dial somewhere reading:

93
00:05:09,968 --> 00:05:13,073
language complexity,
seventy-three per cent.

94
00:05:13,523 --> 00:05:18,003
Look at the range humans have produced.
English. Arabic. Mandarin. Hindi.

95
00:05:18,083 --> 00:05:19,893
Japanese. Swahili.

96
00:05:20,618 --> 00:05:22,968
A language can have punishing verb endings

97
00:05:23,318 --> 00:05:25,608
and a word order you
could set your watch by.

98
00:05:26,410 --> 00:05:30,520
Another can have almost no endings
and a syntax that forgives nothing.

99
00:05:31,263 --> 00:05:33,883
One may have a plain tense
system and an elaborate

100
00:05:33,963 --> 00:05:36,633
way of marking how you
know what you are claiming.

101
00:05:37,397 --> 00:05:40,177
And some will pack into a
single word what another

102
00:05:40,257 --> 00:05:42,567
language would spread
across half a sentence.

103
00:05:43,509 --> 00:05:47,079
Whatever you measure, you are measuring
one room of a very large house.

104
00:05:49,819 --> 00:05:52,209
Which brings us back to our ancient human.

105
00:05:53,008 --> 00:05:54,908
Latin nouns had a set of case endings.

106
00:05:54,988 --> 00:05:57,168
Six of them.

107
00:05:57,248 --> 00:06:01,638
Or five, or seven, depending on which
grammar book you happen to open.

108
00:06:02,388 --> 00:06:04,588
And when linguists compare
the descendants and work

109
00:06:04,668 --> 00:06:08,758
backwards, the ancestor they
arrive at looks elaborate too.

110
00:06:11,641 --> 00:06:13,891
Proto-Indo-European. Nobody
has ever found a text in it.

111
00:06:14,571 --> 00:06:17,041
It is a reconstruction, not a record.

112
00:06:17,691 --> 00:06:20,841
Assembled by comparing the
languages that came out of it.

113
00:06:23,111 --> 00:06:26,321
So why would anyone that long
ago need grammar like that?

114
00:06:27,090 --> 00:06:31,980
Because a society without technology
is not a society without complications.

115
00:06:33,138 --> 00:06:37,768
Imagine having to say: "The man you saw
by the river yesterday took my spear."

116
00:06:38,610 --> 00:06:39,539
Who.

117
00:06:39,619 --> 00:06:40,819
Which man.

118
00:06:40,942 --> 00:06:41,972
When.

119
00:06:42,052 --> 00:06:43,252
Where.

120
00:06:43,495 --> 00:06:44,695
Whose spear.

121
00:06:45,385 --> 00:06:47,985
And a question that some
languages oblige you to answer

122
00:06:48,065 --> 00:06:52,275
every time: did you see it
happen, or did somebody tell you?

123
00:06:52,905 --> 00:06:56,525
Get that wrong in some languages
and you have not been impolite.

124
00:06:56,985 --> 00:06:58,395
You have been ungrammatical.

125
00:06:59,254 --> 00:07:02,504
Language is not mainly a tool
for describing technology.

126
00:07:02,874 --> 00:07:05,744
It is a tool for handling other people.

127
00:07:06,477 --> 00:07:11,267
And people were never simple. We make
promises. We tell stories. We negotiate.

128
00:07:11,517 --> 00:07:14,127
We remember. We gossip. We lie.

129
00:07:14,961 --> 00:07:16,511
No smartphone required.

130
00:07:19,601 --> 00:07:21,681
All of which makes language look much less

131
00:07:21,761 --> 00:07:24,651
like engineering, and
much more like evolution.

132
00:07:25,649 --> 00:07:27,839
Evolution doesn't design anything.

133
00:07:28,609 --> 00:07:30,719
It edits whatever is already there.

134
00:07:31,443 --> 00:07:32,913
It keeps the compromises.

135
00:07:33,763 --> 00:07:35,113
It carries the leftovers.

136
00:07:35,703 --> 00:07:39,813
It is stuck with decisions made
by creatures that died long ago.

137
00:07:40,567 --> 00:07:42,697
Languages behave the same way.

138
00:07:43,367 --> 00:07:45,947
They inherit structures,
bend them, mislay a few,

139
00:07:46,027 --> 00:07:48,817
and keep the strange ones
entirely by accident.

140
00:07:49,775 --> 00:07:51,205
"Went" is a leftover.

141
00:07:51,895 --> 00:07:53,785
Silent letters are leftovers.

142
00:07:54,595 --> 00:07:57,765
Irregular verbs are a museum
that every child has to memorise.

143
00:08:00,627 --> 00:08:03,737
And that leads to the part
people find hardest to accept.

144
00:08:04,691 --> 00:08:05,981
There is no direction.

145
00:08:09,111 --> 00:08:11,701
No rule says a language
ought to get simpler.

146
00:08:12,491 --> 00:08:14,421
No rule says it ought to get richer.

147
00:08:15,246 --> 00:08:17,496
The Slavic languages show it neatly.

148
00:08:18,306 --> 00:08:20,356
Most of them kept a rich case system.

149
00:08:21,211 --> 00:08:24,951
Bulgarian lost the old one on its
nouns almost completely, and grew

150
00:08:25,031 --> 00:08:29,621
something else instead: a definite
article stuck on the end of the word.

151
00:08:30,373 --> 00:08:35,003
Same ancestor. Opposite directions.
Neither one is the upgrade.

152
00:08:37,383 --> 00:08:40,093
And the same thing happened
right across the world.

153
00:08:40,871 --> 00:08:43,021
People separate. Languages drift.

154
00:08:44,018 --> 00:08:47,308
Sometimes they meet again and
start borrowing from each other.

155
00:08:47,997 --> 00:08:51,947
It was never a ladder. It is a
tree, and it is still growing.

156
00:08:55,357 --> 00:08:58,157
Latin was not complicated
because the Romans

157
00:08:58,237 --> 00:09:00,727
had discovered a superior
way to communicate.

158
00:09:01,767 --> 00:09:05,097
And modern English is not
simpler because we are cleverer.

159
00:09:05,703 --> 00:09:07,913
They are different outcomes
of different histories.

160
00:09:08,978 --> 00:09:11,588
So perhaps the question
was wrong from the start.

161
00:09:12,466 --> 00:09:15,616
Instead of asking why some
languages are so complicated,

162
00:09:15,946 --> 00:09:18,736
we could ask why we ever
expected a simple one.

163
00:09:21,046 --> 00:09:22,426
Language was never designed.

164
00:09:22,506 --> 00:09:25,726
No plan. No final version.

165
00:09:25,806 --> 00:09:27,636
No committee tidying up the mess.

166
00:09:28,461 --> 00:09:32,031
It only has to work well enough for
people to understand each other.

167
00:09:32,861 --> 00:09:34,811
Everything after that is history.

168
00:09:35,663 --> 00:09:37,973
Language doesn't evolve
towards perfection.

169
00:09:38,983 --> 00:09:41,513
It evolves towards
whatever people keep using.
